Introduction
The movement and storage of hazardous materials are very important to our energy system, yet they pose risks, notably from BLEVEs (Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapour Explosions). BLEVEs occur when pressurised gas containers burst, releasing a massive amount of energy suddenly. Predicting BLEVEs is important for safety. Our project employs machine learning to predict the peak pressure of BLEVE explosions by analysing data from a simulated scenario. The aim of this report is to discuss the method used to create a model that can accurately predict the peak pressures of a BLEVE at different positions on an obstacle. The project involves taking in given data and an expected resulting pressure and training a model to be able to take in new data and predict the pressure that is not given. The results of the model would be compared against other models in a competition to see who can create the best model.

Data Preprocessing
Data standardisation is crucial for refining datasets before deploying machine learning models, ensuring uniformity and compatibility. The process involved fixing discrepancies, addressing missing data, outliers, and
duplicates, laying a good foundation for predictive models.

