GBMs showed a small improvement over the previously constructed Random Forest model. This was most likely due to their ability to dynamically focus on harder-to-predict instances and effectively capture complex, non-linear interactions within the dataset. Due to overfitting with hyperparameter tuning, little was done to modify the model. It did outperform all other models tested and was the leading model for the competition submissions. The data going into it seemed to be the most important step so this was focussed on instead.

Conclusion

The ability to efficiently organise and manage code is fundamental in machine learning projects, especially when experimenting with multiple models. In this project, a variety of models were tested including Linear Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forests, XGBoost, Gradient Boosting, SVM, and LightGBM. This diverse approach was made possible by structuring the code in a way that allowed easy swapping and testing of different models.This was also made easier by python's ability to simply import different libraries and with use of ChatGpt to quickly receive suggested models and construct quick functions to test them.

One significant breakthrough in the project was the implementation of polynomial feature transformations, which greatly impacted the predictive accuracy of the different models. By creating polynomial features, they could capture more complex interactions between variables, leading to a substantial improvement in model performance. The highest score achieved was 0.22265, a testament to the effectiveness of enhancing feature complexity.

However, the use of polynomial features introduced computational challenges, which had a huge impact on hyperparameter tuning. Due to the increased computation, some models required up to an hour to complete. This complexity led us to a shift towards optimising initial features and using polynomial transformations rather than focusing solely on hyperparameter tuning. The limitation of submitting only five entries per day also outlined the importance of making each model iteration count, prioritising input data and substantial modifications over incremental tuning.

This project showed the importance of code organisation in machine learning workflows and the strategic balance between feature engineering and hyperparameter tuning. There was a solid understanding discovered in the importance of bias and variance and the impact on trained and test data. By prioritising the modification of input data through polynomial features, the impact of each submission was maximised, leading to more efficient and effective model optimization.
